\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Finite cyclic duality with every torus sector}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{For the [cyclic lattice data](fdip-00NL), set #{r=d-2}
and choose a cocycle #{h_s\in Z^r(K^*;A)} representing
each #{s\in H^r(K^*;A)}. Define the dual weight function
on #{C^r(K^*;A)} by
##{\widehat W(q)=\prod_{p\in K_2}
      \widehat w_p((D_2^{-1}q)_p).}
Then the normalized link partition function satisfies
##{\begin{aligned}
 Z&=\sum_{b\in Z_2(K;A)}\prod_p\widehat w_p(b_p)\\
  &=\frac1{|Z^{r-1}(K^*;A)|}
       \sum_{s\in H^r(K^*;A)}
       \sum_{\alpha\in C^{r-1}(K^*;A)}
           \widehat W(\delta\alpha+h_s).
\end{aligned}}
Equivalently, with normalized counting on dual cochains,
##{Z=|B^r(K^*;A)|\sum_{s\in H^r(K^*;A)}
 \left[N^{-n_{r-1}^*}\sum_{\alpha\in C^{r-1}(K^*;A)}
              \widehat W(\delta\alpha+h_s)\right].}
The outer operation is a sum over #{N^{\binom d2}} sectors.
Both expressions are independent of their cocycle representatives.
All displayed integer inverses exist in #{R} by
\ref{fdip-00NN}. This is the finite-cochain form of the cyclic
Fourier duality discussed in
\citet{Section 6, pp. 12–13}{wegner2014duality}, with
normalization and topology proved explicitly here.}

\proof{
\p{Apply \ref{fdip-00NJ} to every face and exchange finite
sums. Label the chosen face characters by the chain
#{b=\sum_p b_pp\in C_2(K;A)}. The pairing identity gives
##{\begin{aligned}
 Z&=\sum_{b\in C_2(K;A)}\left(\prod_p\widehat w_p(b_p)\right)
     N^{-n_1}\sum_{a\in C^1(K;A)}
          \zeta^{\langle b,\delta a\rangle},\\
 \langle b,\delta a\rangle&=\langle\partial b,a\rangle.
\end{aligned}}
The remaining link average factors as
##{\prod_{e\in K_1}
   \left[N^{-1}\sum_{x\in A}\zeta^{(\partial b)_e x}\right]
   =\mathbf1_{\partial b=0}.}
Every factor #{N} from a surviving character sum cancels its
normalized link factor. This proves the cycle formula without
any residual division by an original gauge-group volume.}

\p{By \ref{fdip-00NM}, #{D_2} maps those cycles bijectively
to #{Z^r(K^*;A)}. Each cocycle #{q} has a unique class #{s},
and #{q-h_s\in B^r}, so #{q=\delta\alpha+h_s} for some
#{\alpha}. Two choices of #{\alpha} give the same #{q}
exactly when their difference belongs to #{Z^{r-1}}.
Thus the parameterization counts every #{q} precisely
#{|Z^{r-1}|} times. Dividing by this unit proves the
second expression. If #{h'_s=h_s+\delta\beta_s}, the
translation #{\alpha\mapsto\alpha-\beta_s} leaves every
argument unchanged, proving representative independence.}

\p{Finally \ref{fdip-00NN} gives
#{|B^r|=N^{n_{r-1}^*}/|Z^{r-1}|}.
Extracting the normalized dual-cochain average therefore gives
the last formula. There is no division by the number of
cohomology classes.}
}
